.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt SYSCALLS 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm syscalls
.Nd system call interface and number index
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Dl int 0x80
.Dl syscall
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Rach2 uses Linux's system call numbers and register interface for the
machine's architecture.
On i686 a program executes
.Ql int 0x80
with the i386 system call number in EAX, and the arguments, in order, in
EBX, ECX, EDX, ESI, EDI and EBP.
On x86-64 it executes
.Ql syscall
with the x86-64 number in RAX, and the arguments in RDI, RSI, RDX, R10, R8
and R9; the instruction keeps RCX and R11 for the return address and the
flags.
The result is returned in EAX or RAX; a failure is a negative error number.
Musl uses its Linux system call layer of either architecture unchanged.
.Pp
A page's numbers and registers are the i386 interface's.
On x86-64 a call takes its number from the second table below and its
arguments, in the order the page gives them, in the x86-64 registers; a
page says where x86-64 has the call under another name or with another
structure.
.Pp
This section describes the calls implemented by Rach2.
A number not registered in the kernel's dispatch table returns
.Er ENOSYS ,
including Linux calls not implemented here.
A registered number does not imply that every Linux option or behavior
is implemented; the individual pages describe each call.
.Pp
Where a page says that a call needs effective user ID 0, it means a
privileged thread: one whose effective user ID is 0 and which has not
given up its capabilities with
.Xr capset 2 .
Linux asks for a capability in each such place, and this kernel keeps
every capability or none.
